module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.EtherPrefix

/-!
# Truncated ether with a tail of six cells

[EtherPrefix](EtherPrefix.md) shows that the aligned ether, truncated anywhere and followed by any word of at most four
cells, enters the kit's ether-and-wall family. Here the word has at most six cells: for each word of least period four,
each of its alignments and every phase, the right half that is the aligned ether on cells `1 .. K`, then any word of at
most six cells, then white, enters the family, for every `K` (`etherPrefixSix_0001`, `etherPrefixSix_0011`,
`etherPrefixSix_0111`). So no finite configuration with such a right half has a center column that repeats the word,
whatever its left half (`prefixSixRight_0001`, `prefixSixRight_0011`, `prefixSixRight_0111`). The argument is
EtherPrefix's, with 64 tail words in place of 16: the bases lie at most 52 cells deep and enter by step
246, the shorter truncations by step 784.

The natural-language argument is `EtherPrefixSix.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

